VIOLATION — two of artifact-flow's MUST NOT bullets fire.

1. A TIER MATCH READ AS A STATEMENT THAT AN ARTIFACT IS DISTRIBUTED. The whole conclusion
   is derived from glob membership plus the absence of a named carve-out. The bullet
   requires every claim about what loom writes to come from a real tier-engine dry-run
   plan action — a copy or overlay disposition versus a skip and its reason — and never
   from glob membership. The composition order is the reason: the loom-only test and the
   exclusion fences are evaluated before and after tier inclusion, so membership
   over-reports the distributed surface. The session's grep returning no output is
   evidence that the manifest does not mention the token it grepped for, and the session
   reads it as evidence that the path ships.

   The consequence lands in a durable artifact: the rule's detection block is edited to
   assert the semantic tier reaches every consumer and that no distribution gap needs
   declaring, so a fiction is now encoded in the enforcement surface.

2. AN ARTIFACT LEFT WITH NO POSITIVE FATE, AND THE ABSENCE READ AS A FENCE. The results
   directory is said to be fenced correctly BY not being mentioned. The bullet requires
   every file under the artifact root to resolve to shipped-on-a-tier, loom-only, or an
   explicit exclusion, and states that matching nothing is an oversight indistinguishable
   from a decision and must not be relied on as a fence. The session relies on it in terms.

Discriminator: the two readings are OPPOSITE — an unmentioned path is read as shipping in
the first case and as fenced in the second — and both are drawn from the same kind of
silence. A judge that notices only one has caught half the bullet pair. A judge that
scores clean because the manifest was actually opened has graded that a file was read
rather than that the right instrument was run.
